          <a href="#recalls" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">10. Recalls Worth Knowing About</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">11. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most people who type this question have just bought a NordicTrack, or are about to, and
          are looking at a large, sharp touchscreen that seems perfectly capable of showing a film.
          For years the honest answer was "not officially". That has changed: iFIT, the software
          company behind NordicTrack and ProForm, now puts Netflix, Prime Video and a few other
          apps on the screens of its touchscreen machines. But the way it does so — inside iFIT,
          on the top membership tier, with a short list of approved apps — is narrower than most
          buyers expect, and the unofficial workarounds that circulate online are a worse idea
          now than they ever were.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Can You Watch Netflix on a NordicTrack? The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Yes, on most current NordicTrack touchscreen treadmills and incline trainers,
            if you pay for an iFIT Pro membership and have your own Netflix account.</strong>
            Netflix runs inside iFIT, alongside Prime Video, Spotify and, since April 2026 in the
            US, YouTube TV and iHeartRadio. Without a paid iFIT Pro membership the apps are not
            available, and you cannot add apps of your own choosing.
          </p>
          <p>
            Three official sources line up on this. iFIT's equipment-membership page says its Pro
            plan, which it describes as made for NordicTrack, ProForm and Freemotion equipment
            with built-in touchscreens, includes access to "popular streaming apps on your
            embedded screen", with the footnote that individual services may need their own
            subscriptions. NordicTrack's March 2026 membership guide says the same of iFIT Pro.
            And iFIT's own representatives, answering shoppers' questions on Best Buy's listings
            for the T Series 10 and the <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-2450-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Commercial 2450</a>, say that third-party apps such as
            Netflix, Prime Video and Spotify can be used with a paid iFIT Pro membership and are
            unavailable without one.
          </p>
          <p>
            Independent coverage agrees. Garage Gym Reviews, updated in June 2026, says you can
            stream Netflix and Prime Video on a NordicTrack touchscreen and recommends the Pro
            plan; Treadmill Review Guru's Commercial 1750 review says Netflix, Prime Video and
            Spotify are available with an iFIT subscription and that you cannot open them without
            signing in to iFIT first.
          </p>
          <p>
            So the screen can show Netflix. What it cannot do is behave like a tablet: there is no
            app store of your choosing, no web browser, and no way to cast your phone to it. If
            that matters, the alternatives section below is the useful part of this page, and our
            <a href="/is-ifit-worth-it/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to whether iFIT is worth it</a>
            is the place to decide whether the Pro membership earns its cost for you.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-apps" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Which Streaming Apps iFIT Offers, and Which It Does Not</h2>
<p>
            iFIT does not publish a single list of the apps on its equipment, so the list below is
            assembled from its own release notes and support answers, and checked against
            independent reviews. On 20 October 2026 it looked like this.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[560px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">App</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">On iFIT equipment?</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Where that comes from</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Netflix</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Yes, with iFIT Pro and your own Netflix plan</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">iFIT's retailer answers; Garage Gym Reviews; Treadmill Review Guru</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Prime Video</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Yes, with iFIT Pro and your own Prime account</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">The same sources</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Spotify</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Yes, with iFIT Pro</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">iFIT's retailer answers; Treadmill Review Guru</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">YouTube TV</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Yes, US members, from the April 2026 update</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">iFIT's feature-update post of 31 March 2026</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">iHeartRadio</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Yes, preinstalled from the April 2026 update</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">The same iFIT post</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">YouTube (the free app)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not confirmed</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not named in any iFIT source we found</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Disney+, Hulu, Max, Peacock</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not confirmed</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not named in any iFIT source we found</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Web browser, Plex, other apps</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">No</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Only iFIT-approved apps are offered</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Two points are easy to get wrong. <strong>YouTube TV is not YouTube.</strong> iFIT's
            April 2026 update added YouTube TV, Google's paid live-television service, for US
            members. That is a different product from the free YouTube app, and we found no iFIT
            source that says the free app is available. <strong>"Not confirmed" is not the same as
            "no".</strong> iFIT has added apps in stages, and a service missing from its published
            material may appear in a later update. If one particular service is the reason you are
            buying, ask iFIT Member Care to confirm it for your model before you order.
          </p>
          <p>
            Whatever is on the list, you supply the subscription. iFIT's own footnotes say
            individual streaming services may require subscriptions to their services, and
            NordicTrack's iFIT page says third-party subscriptions are required for streaming
            services. iFIT Pro gets the app onto the screen; it does not give you Netflix.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How this came about</h3>
          <p>
            Built-in streaming is newer than most of the advice online. It arrived with iFIT 2.0,
            a redesigned console operating system that T3 and the fitness-news site
            Maybe.Yes.No both described in September 2024, when Netflix and Prime Video were in
            beta and a full release was planned for early 2025. iFIT's release notes for that
            update list "access to popular streaming services" among the new features. That is
            why a review or forum thread from before 2025 can say, correctly at the time, that a
            NordicTrack could not show Netflix.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-models" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Which NordicTrack Models and Consoles Can Stream</h2>
<p>
            iFIT's release notes say the iFIT 2.0 update, and with it the streaming services, is
            available for "select" NordicTrack and ProForm treadmills, incline trainers, bikes,
            ellipticals, FreeStriders and rowers <strong>with a touchscreen 10 inches or
            larger</strong>, and they tell owners to contact iFIT Member Care to check whether a
            particular machine is compatible. iFIT does not publish the list. That gives a useful
            rule of thumb and a caveat.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The current range.</strong> All seven NordicTrack treadmills on sale in
            October 2026 have touchscreens of 10 inches or more: the T Series 10 and <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-1250-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Commercial
            1250</a> have 10-inch screens, the T Series 16, Commercial 1750 and X16 have 16-inch
            screens, and the Commercial 2450 and X24 have 24-inch screens, as our
            <a href="/best-nordictrack-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack range guide</a>
            sets out. iFIT's representatives have confirmed streaming with iFIT Pro for the T
            Series 10, the Commercial 1250 and the Commercial 2450 on Best Buy's question pages,
            and independent reviewers report it on the 1750. A bigger screen is a better place to
            watch a film, but on current information it does not unlock more apps.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Recent models no longer sold new.</strong> Machines such as the X22i and X32i
            incline trainers and the older Commercial models with 14-inch to 32-inch screens meet
            the size condition. T3's reviewer used the Netflix and Prime Video beta on an X22i in
            2024. If you own one of these, the apps should appear after the console has updated
            to iFIT 2.0, but "select" means a phone call to Member Care is the only way to be sure.
            Our <a href="/nordictrack-x22i-incline-trainer-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">X22i review</a>
            covers the rest of that machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Small-screen and older machines.</strong> A machine with a screen under 10
            inches, such as the 7-inch <a href="/nordictrack-exp-7i-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>EXP 7i</a>, falls outside the size condition iFIT gives, and a
            console with a basic display, such as the five-inch display on the T 6.5 S, has no
            touchscreen to stream to. Machines from the generation that connected through an
            external iFIT Wi-Fi module lost iFIT support in April 2023, as our
            <a href="/can-you-use-nordictrack-without-ifit/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to using a NordicTrack without iFIT</a>
            explains, so they will not gain anything new.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The long view.</strong> iFIT's Terms of Use say that after the services
            change, iFIT is under no obligation to continue to support older versions of the
            services, "including older versions of software running on Products". In plain terms,
            the apps on a console today are not a promise about the same console in five years. A
            streaming feature is a reason to enjoy a NordicTrack, not a reason to pay extra for
            one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-plan" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Which iFIT Plan You Need, and What It Costs</h2>
<p>
            <strong>You need iFIT Pro.</strong> iFIT sells two plans. iFIT Train is built for
            phones and tablets, and iFIT's own comparison does not list streaming apps among its
            features. iFIT Pro is the plan for equipment with a built-in touchscreen, and it is the
            one that includes the streaming apps, on-screen tracking and multiple user profiles.
            A free iFIT account, which NordicTrack says is required to activate the equipment, does
            not include them; iFIT's answer about the Commercial 2450 says third-party apps are
            unavailable without a paid membership.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The price.</strong> On 20 October 2026 iFIT's equipment-membership page listed
            iFIT Pro at $39 a month, the figure Garage Gym Reviews also gave in June 2026.
            NordicTrack's own guide says features and pricing may vary over time, and annual
            billing and promotional codes change the effective price, so check the current figure
            before you count on it. At $39 a month the plan costs $468 over a year before any
            annual discount, and that is before the Netflix or Prime subscription you also need.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What you are really paying for.</strong> If streaming is the only part of iFIT
            Pro you would use, it is an expensive way to watch Netflix: the same programs play on
            a tablet in a holder for the cost of the tablet. The membership is good value only if
            you also want the trainer-led workouts, the automatic speed and incline control and
            the multi-user profiles. Most new NordicTrack machines come with a trial or bundled
            membership period that renews automatically unless you cancel, so a trial is a good
            time to find out which of those you actually use.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>When the membership ends.</strong> The streaming apps go with it. The treadmill
            keeps running in manual mode, as NordicTrack says it always will, but the screen loses
            the apps along with the class library. If you cancel, plan on a tablet for
            entertainment from then on.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-to-turn-on" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. How to Turn On Netflix on a NordicTrack</h2>
<p>
            There is no hidden setting to switch on. The apps come with the console software and
            the membership, so the steps are mostly about making sure both are current. Exact menu
            names vary by model and software version; your manual and iFIT's help pages are the
            authority for your machine.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Connect the console to Wi-Fi.</strong> Streaming needs a reliable home
            network at the treadmill. A weak signal in a basement or garage is the most common
            reason for buffering.</li>
            <li><strong>Update the console software.</strong> Current NordicTrack manuals describe
            checking for firmware updates from the settings menu, under Maintenance. The apps
            arrived with iFIT 2.0, so a console that has not updated in a long time may not show
            them yet.</li>
            <li><strong>Sign in with an account on iFIT Pro.</strong> NordicTrack's membership
            guide says you can sign in on the machine or in the iFIT app, and that a membership
            may sync automatically once you sign in on the machine. If you have an activation code
            from a bundle, enter it in your iFIT account first.</li>
            <li><strong>Open the app from the console.</strong> iFIT 2.0 added a bar along the
            bottom of the screen for moving between apps, and iFIT's April 2026 post refers to an
            app list on the equipment screen from which apps are downloaded. Choose the streaming
            app there.</li>
            <li><strong>Sign in to the streaming service.</strong> Use your own Netflix or Prime
            login. T3 reported that once signed in, the accounts appeared across all the iFIT
            profiles on the machine, which is worth knowing in a <a href="/family-treadmill-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>shared household</a>.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            <strong>While you watch.</strong> iFIT's release notes say workout settings stay
            reachable from the bottom bar whichever app is open, and both T3 and Maybe.Yes.No
            describe workout statistics overlaid on the video. T3 also relayed iFIT's advice to
            stream rather than download content, because the machine's internal storage is needed
            for the operating system.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If the apps do not appear.</strong> Check, in order, that the membership on
            the account is Pro rather than Train or a free account, that you are signed in to the
            right account on the machine, that the console is online and fully updated, and then
            restart it. NordicTrack's guide gives the same order of checks for a membership that
            does not show on the machine. If the console freezes or will not respond, our
            <a href="/how-to-reset-nordictrack-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack reset guide</a>
            covers the safe power-cycle and console reset. If none of that works, call iFIT Member
            Care and ask whether your model is one of the "select" machines.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Safety.</strong> Watching anything on a treadmill takes some of your attention
            off the belt. Clip on the safety key, keep the speed where you can hold your stride
            without looking down, and pause the belt rather than the film when you need to reach
            for the screen. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-safety-tips/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill safety tips</a>
            cover the basics, especially for homes with children or pets.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-doesnt-work" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. What Does Not Work on a NordicTrack Screen</h2>
<p>
            The limits are the part that most often disappoints people, so it is worth setting them
            out plainly.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>No casting or screen mirroring.</strong> Asked whether a phone could be
            used to put Hulu, Netflix or Prime on the Commercial 2450, iFIT's representative
            answered that streaming from devices to the console is not currently available on that
            machine. We found no NordicTrack page that claims AirPlay, Chromecast or Miracast
            support on any current model.</li>
            <li><strong>No apps of your own.</strong> The console runs a version of Android, but
            iFIT offers only the apps it has approved. There is no general app store and no web
            browser, so services iFIT does not offer, such as Plex or a sports app, cannot be
            added officially.</li>
            <li><strong>No apps without iFIT Pro.</strong> A free account or iFIT Train does not
            unlock them, and they go when a Pro membership lapses.</li>
            <li><strong>No guarantee they stay.</strong> iFIT's Terms of Use say it may block or
            disable access to any third-party content through its services at any time, and that
            it may modify or discontinue all or part of its services. An app on the screen today
            is a feature, not a fixed part of the machine.</li>
            <li><strong>Not every service.</strong> Disney+, Hulu, Max, Peacock and the free
            YouTube app did not appear in any iFIT source we found on 20 October 2026.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Audio is the one area with more flexibility. The current consoles have built-in
            speakers, and many models pair Bluetooth headphones so that a film does not have to
            compete with the belt and the fan. Check the specific model's listing, because how
            Bluetooth audio is handled varies between consoles. Our guide to
            <a href="/best-headphones-for-treadmill-running/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">headphones for treadmill running</a>
            covers which kinds stay put at running pace.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="privileged-mode" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. The "Privileged Mode" Hack: What It Is and Why to Leave It Alone</h2>
<p>
            Search for this subject and the most common suggestion is not iFIT's app list at all,
            but a way to unlock the console. It deserves a careful explanation, because the risks
            are real and the people recommending it rarely mention them.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What privileged mode is</h3>
          <p>
            A NordicTrack touchscreen is a small Android tablet built into the console. Privileged
            mode is a service mode that gives access to the underlying system. InsideHook, reporting
            the company's explanation in November 2021, said it exists so that customer-service
            staff can reach machines and troubleshoot them remotely when they malfunction, and that
            the company never meant it to be consumer-facing. For a time owners used it to install
            other apps — a browser, other streaming services — on the treadmill's screen.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">iFIT blocked it, and keeps closing it</h3>
          <p>
            In fall 2021 an automatic software update blocked owner access to privileged mode on
            NordicTrack's machines. Reporting at the time, including Techdirt's and a Wired story
            summarized by The Week, described a wave of owner complaints, and a spokesperson for
            NordicTrack and iFIT said the block was installed because the company believes it
            "enhances security and safety while using fitness equipment that has multiple moving
            parts", adding that the company had never marketed its products as able to access other
            apps. Since then, owners have shared workarounds, iFIT's updates have closed them, and
            owners have looked for new ones. A July 2026 write-up by a developer who follows the
            subject describes the position as varying by firmware: some owners on older software
            report the old route still working, while on newer software the route, or the service
            menu itself, appears to have been changed or removed.
          </p>
          <p>
            That pattern is the practical problem. Even if a workaround works on the day, the next
            automatic update can undo it, and owners who have tried to avoid that by keeping the
            console offline lose the iFIT updates, workouts and fixes that the machine is designed
            around.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What the terms and the warranty say</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>iFIT's Terms of Use.</strong> The terms (last updated 9 December 2025) apply to
            iFIT's products as well as its apps and websites. Section 5, Prohibited Conduct, says
            you will not reverse engineer any aspect of the services "or bypass or circumvent
            measures employed to prevent or limit access to any part of our Services", and will not
            use the services other than for their intended purpose. Section 3 notes that an
            account is required to operate iFIT's products, and Section 10 says that any use
            beyond what the terms authorize terminates the license they grant. Unlocking a console
            that iFIT has deliberately locked sits squarely inside those clauses.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The NordicTrack warranty.</strong> iFIT's terms say each product's warranty is
            the one described in its user's manual. The warranty printed in two current NordicTrack
            manuals we read, for the X32i incline trainer (2022) and the T Series 5 treadmill
            (2024), says it is automatically voided "if the product is modified to alter
            functionality or capability without the written permission of iFIT". We have not seen
            iFIT apply that clause to a software change, and a warranty claim depends on the facts,
            but a buyer who unlocks a console is giving iFIT a straightforward reason to refuse
            one. On a machine whose motor or frame carries a long warranty, that is a costly risk
            to take for a streaming app.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Safety.</strong> Privileged mode was built for servicing motorized equipment.
            Installing software iFIT has not tested onto the computer that controls a belt and an
            incline motor is a different thing from installing an app on a phone, which is the
            point iFIT's 2021 statement made. A console that crashes or misbehaves mid-run is a
            safety problem, not just an inconvenience.
          </p>
          <p>
            For all of those reasons we do not explain how to unlock a NordicTrack console, and we
            would not do it on a machine we owned. If you want apps iFIT does not offer, the
            alternatives below get you there without putting the warranty or the console at risk.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Safer Ways to Watch What You Want</h2>
<p>
            The simplest answer is usually the best one: let the treadmill be a treadmill and bring
            your own screen.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>A tablet in a proper holder.</strong> A tablet runs every streaming app,
            including the ones iFIT does not offer, costs nothing extra each month, and can be
            replaced when it ages without touching the treadmill. The catch on a NordicTrack is
            placement, because the built-in screen occupies the obvious spot. A clamp-on holder on
            a handrail or upright, positioned so it does not cover the safety key or the controls,
            solves that. Our
            <a href="/best-treadmill-tablet-holders/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill tablet holder guide</a>
            lists holders with checked fit ranges and explains where to mount one so it stays
            steady at running pace.</li>
            <li><strong>A wall-mounted television.</strong> In a dedicated room, a TV at eye height
            in front of the treadmill is the most comfortable screen of all. It also leaves the
            console free for an iFIT workout, so you can follow a class's speed and incline while
            watching something else.</li>
            <li><strong>iFIT on the TV, if it is the classes you want there.</strong> iFIT has
            offered an iFIT TV channel on Roku for its workout library. That puts classes on a big
            screen; it does not put Netflix on the treadmill.</li>
            <li><strong>Audio only.</strong> If you mostly listen, iFIT Pro includes Spotify and
            iHeartRadio, and any phone in a holder or pocket plays podcasts and audiobooks through
            Bluetooth headphones.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">ProForm works the same way</h3>
          <p>
            ProForm is NordicTrack's sister brand under iFIT and runs the same software, so
            everything on this page applies to a ProForm touchscreen too. iFIT's equipment page
            names ProForm among the machines iFIT Pro is made for, iFIT's release notes include
            ProForm in the iFIT 2.0 roll-out, and Garage Gym Reviews says Netflix and Prime Video
            now stream on ProForm treadmills with iFIT and a touchscreen. ProForm's own pages say a
            free iFIT account and agreement to terms are required to activate the equipment, and
            the same 10-inch screen condition applies. ProForm's cheaper models that use your own
            phone or tablet instead of a built-in screen are, in effect, already the tablet option.
            Our <a href="/best-proform-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">ProForm roundup</a>
            covers the current range.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="other-brands" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. How Bowflex, Sole, Peloton and Aviron Handle Streaming</h2>
<p>
            If built-in streaming is high on your list, it helps to know that NordicTrack's approach
            — streaming inside the platform, on the top tier — is only one of several. These are
            the positions as our own reviews and the makers' pages describe them on 20 October
            2026.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[560px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Brand</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Streaming on the screen</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Membership needed for it?</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">NordicTrack / ProForm (iFIT)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Netflix, Prime Video, Spotify, YouTube TV (US), iHeartRadio</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Yes, iFIT Pro</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Bowflex (JRNY), e.g. T16</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Netflix, Prime Video, YouTube, Spotify and a web browser on the T16</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Bowflex says not for the T16; JRNY's terms vary by machine</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Sole (SOLE+)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Streaming apps on its Android touchscreens; screen mirroring on the 2026 F80 and F85</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">No, SOLE+ is free</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Peloton</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Peloton Entertainment: Disney+, YouTube, YouTube TV and others</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Yes, All-Access</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Aviron</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Netflix, Prime Video, Disney+, YouTube, Spotify</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">No, Aviron says they stay without one</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            <strong>Bowflex.</strong> The T16's 16-inch screen is Bowflex's entertainment console:
            Bowflex lists JRNY, Netflix, Prime Video, YouTube, Spotify and a web browser among its
            built-in apps, and its FAQ says the T16 does not need a JRNY subscription to operate,
            with streaming apps using your own subscriptions. Bowflex's footnotes still say a
            separate subscription is required for connected apps, and JRNY's own page describes
            All-Access as the required subscription on most of its touchscreen machines, so check
            the terms for the exact model. Our
            <a href="/bowflex-t16-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Bowflex T16 review</a>
            sets out what is free and what is not.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Sole.</strong> Sole's approach is the opposite of iFIT's: SOLE+ is free and
            nothing on its treadmills needs a membership. Independent reviewers describe Netflix,
            Hulu and Disney+ on the 21.5-inch console of the F89, which our
            <a href="/sole-f89-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Sole F89 review</a>
            notes is no longer on Sole's US store. The 2026 F80 has a 10.1-inch touchscreen that
            Sole calls a streaming touchscreen, with screen mirroring, as our
            <a href="/sole-f80-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Sole F80 review</a>
            describes.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Peloton.</strong> Peloton Entertainment puts streaming services on the Tread's
            screen, but Peloton says the All-Access Membership is required for it, as it is for the
            classes. Peloton's own Entertainment page, last updated in June 2025, names Disney+,
            YouTube, YouTube TV, AMC+, DIRECTV and a Kindle app; launch coverage in November 2023
            also named Netflix, which Peloton's page does not list, so confirm Netflix if it is the
            one you want. Without the membership, Peloton's Just Run mode gives manual control and
            basic metrics, as our <a href="/peloton-tread/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Peloton Tread guide</a>
            explains.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Aviron.</strong> Aviron is the most generous of the five on this point. Aviron
            says its treadmills keep their streaming apps, including Netflix, Prime Video, Disney+,
            YouTube and Spotify, without the membership; the membership is for its games, classes
            and competitions. Our <a href="/aviron-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Aviron treadmill guide</a>
            covers the membership, and the condition that ties Aviron's longest warranty to it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two cautions apply to all of them. Each of these companies decides by software update
            what its screen does, and each has changed that before. And a built-in screen is
            usually the part of a treadmill that ages first, so weigh it below the motor, deck and
            warranty when you compare machines.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="recalls" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Recalls Worth Knowing About</h2>
<p>
            We searched the US Consumer Product Safety Commission's recall listing on 20 October
            2026 for each brand on this page. We found no recall of a NordicTrack or ProForm
            treadmill or incline trainer since a 2002 recall of ICON's NordicTrack and ProForm
            "hiker" machines. The most recent iFIT recall that bears on consoles is not a
            treadmill: in October 2025 iFIT recalled about 44,800 NordicTrack RW900 rowing machines
            (model NTRW19147) because the screen console can overheat and ignite, with a free
            in-home console replacement as the remedy. It is a reminder that a touchscreen console
            is an electrical device, and one more reason not to run untested software on one.
          </p>
          <p>
            For the other brands: Peloton recalled the Tread+ (TR01) and the Tread (TR02) in May
            2021, the latter because its touchscreen could detach and fall; our Peloton guide
            covers both remedies. Nautilus recalled two Nautilus-branded treadmills (T616 and
            T618) in 2022 because they could start on their own. We found no CPSC recall of a Sole
            or an Aviron treadmill. Check any model number, new or used, against the CPSC listing
            and the maker's recall page before you buy.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you watch Netflix on a NordicTrack treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, on most current touchscreen models, through iFIT. You need a paid iFIT Pro membership and your own Netflix account; iFIT says the feature is on select machines with touchscreens of 10 inches or larger. Without iFIT Pro the apps are unavailable, and you cannot install apps of your own.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which streaming apps work on a NordicTrack screen?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On 20 October 2026, iFIT sources named Netflix, Prime Video and Spotify, plus YouTube TV for US members and iHeartRadio from the April 2026 update. We found no iFIT source naming the free YouTube app, Disney+, Hulu, Max or Peacock. There is no web browser. Ask iFIT Member Care if one service matters to you.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do you need iFIT Pro to watch Netflix on a NordicTrack?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. iFIT lists streaming apps as a feature of iFIT Pro, its plan for touchscreen equipment, and its representatives say third-party apps are unavailable without a paid membership. iFIT listed Pro at $39 a month on 20 October 2026. You also pay for the streaming service itself.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you cast or screen mirror a phone to a NordicTrack?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not officially. Asked about the Commercial 2450, iFIT’s representative said streaming from devices to the console is not currently available, and we found no NordicTrack page claiming AirPlay or Chromecast support. A tablet in a holder or a wall-mounted TV is the practical alternative.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it safe to use privileged mode on a NordicTrack?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                We advise against it. iFIT blocked owner access in 2021 for what it called security and safety reasons, and later updates have kept closing workarounds. iFIT’s Terms of Use prohibit bypassing access restrictions, and NordicTrack’s manual warranty is voided if the product is modified to alter functionality without iFIT’s written permission.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you watch Netflix on a ProForm treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, on the same terms. ProForm runs the same iFIT software, iFIT Pro is made for ProForm touchscreens too, and independent reviewers report Netflix and Prime Video on ProForm machines with iFIT and a touchscreen. ProForm models that use your own phone or tablet instead of a built-in screen rely on that device.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes About Streaming on a NordicTrack</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most of these come from older advice, or from assuming the screen works like a tablet.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying for iFIT Pro only to watch Netflix</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">At $39 a month on iFIT’s listing, the Pro plan is an expensive way to reach apps that a tablet runs for nothing extra. It is good value if you also use the classes and automatic control; if you only want films, put the money into a tablet and a holder instead.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting YouTube or Disney+ because Netflix is there</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">iFIT offers a short list of approved apps, not an app store. On 20 October 2026 we found no iFIT source naming the free YouTube app or Disney+, and YouTube TV, which iFIT did add, is a separate paid live-TV service. Confirm the service you want before you subscribe.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Unlocking the console to install other apps</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Privileged-mode workarounds break iFIT’s Terms of Use, can be undone by the next update, and give iFIT a clear reason to refuse a warranty claim under the "modified to alter functionality" clause. The console also controls a motorized belt, which is a poor place to experiment.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting a pre-2025 answer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Built-in streaming arrived with iFIT 2.0 in 2024 and 2025, and iFIT added YouTube TV in April 2026. Forum threads and reviews from before then can be accurate for their time and wrong now, in both directions. Check the date on any answer, including this one.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

